i need to drop in a new tool i made called 2ram. its used like so. 2ram /data/folder1 , or 2ram /data/folder1 4M , or 2ram /data/folder1 2G , where the folder is the folder to be sent to ram, and the size is the size of that folder. default will be 4MB ramdisk. we will be using rdmount /rd 4M etc etc for this function. where 2ram will invoke it to complete our function . then we will bindmount our new ramdisk, over the existing location were copying it from. there must be a 2ram unload , and a 2ram restore function. unload for when its currently running, to restore mounts and copy data from ram into the mount location safely, and restorem to restore mounts to original form before any changes were made.

Assumption: rdmount <mountpoint> <size> mounts a ramdisk at that mountpoint. Each folder gets its own ramdisk at /rd/<path with / replaced by _>.

This is a new file, so there is nothing to insert into. Replace the whole file from cat > /usr/local/bin/2ram through the final chmod line:

bash
cat > /usr/local/bin/2ram << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash
set -u
RDROOT=/rd
STATE=/var/lib/2ram
DEFSIZE=4M

die(){ echo "2ram: $*" >&2; exit 1; }
usage(){
  echo "usage: 2ram DIR [SIZE]   load DIR into ramdisk (default $DEFSIZE), bind over DIR"
  echo "       2ram unload DIR|all   copy ram data back into DIR, then drop mounts"
  echo "       2ram restore DIR|all  drop mounts, DIR returns to its pre-2ram state (ram changes discarded)"
  echo "       2ram list"
  exit 1
}

[ "$(id -u)" -eq 0 ] || die "run as root"
command -v rdmount >/dev/null 2>&1 || die "rdmount not found"

tobytes(){
  local s=${1^^} n u
  s=${s%B}
  n=${s%[KMG]}
  u=${s#"$n"}
  [[ $n =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]] || return 1
  case $u in
    "") echo "$n";;
    K)  echo $((n*1024));;
    M)  echo $((n*1048576));;
    G)  echo $((n*1073741824));;
    *)  return 1;;
  esac
}

mkid(){ echo "$1" | tr '/' '_' | sed 's/^_//'; }

is_bound(){
  mountpoint -q "$MP" && mountpoint -q "$DIR" || return 1
  [ "$(stat -c %d "$DIR")" = "$(stat -c %d "$MP")" ]
}

do_load(){
  local dir size need used id
  [ -n "${1:-}" ] || usage
  dir=$(realpath -e -- "$1") || die "no such path: $1"
  size=${2:-$DEFSIZE}
  [ -d "$dir" ] || die "not a directory: $dir"
  [ "$dir" != "/" ] || die "refusing /"
  id=$(mkid "$dir")
  [ ! -e "$STATE/$id" ] || die "already loaded: $dir"
  need=$(tobytes "$size") || die "bad size: $size"
  used=$(du -sb -- "$dir" | cut -f1)
  [ "$used" -le "$need" ] || die "data is ${used}B, exceeds $size"
  mkdir -p "$STATE" "$RDROOT/$id" || die "mkdir failed"
  DIR=$dir; MP=$RDROOT/$id; SIZE=$size
  rdmount "$MP" "$size" || { rmdir "$MP" 2>/dev/null; die "rdmount failed"; }
  mountpoint -q "$MP" || { rmdir "$MP" 2>/dev/null; die "$MP not mounted by rdmount"; }
  cp -a -- "$DIR"/. "$MP"/ || { umount "$MP"; rmdir "$MP"; die "copy to ram failed, nothing changed"; }
  diff -rq -- "$DIR" "$MP" >/dev/null 2>&1 || { umount "$MP"; rmdir "$MP"; die "verify failed, nothing changed"; }
  mount --bind "$MP" "$DIR" || { umount "$MP"; rmdir "$MP"; die "bind failed, nothing changed"; }
  printf 'DIR=%q\nMP=%q\nSIZE=%q\n' "$DIR" "$MP" "$SIZE" > "$STATE/$id"
  echo "2ram: $DIR -> $MP ($SIZE), bound"
}

do_unload(){
  command -v rsync >/dev/null 2>&1 || die "rsync required for unload"
  is_bound || die "$DIR not currently bound to $MP (use: 2ram restore $DIR)"
  sync
  umount "$DIR" || die "$DIR busy, still bound, nothing changed"
  if ! rsync -a --delete -- "$MP"/ "$DIR"/; then
    mount --bind "$MP" "$DIR"
    die "copy back failed, re-bound ram copy, no data lost"
  fi
  if [ -n "$(rsync -anc --delete --itemize-changes -- "$MP"/ "$DIR"/)" ]; then
    mount --bind "$MP" "$DIR"
    die "verify failed, re-bound ram copy, no data lost"
  fi
  umount "$MP" || die "data saved to $DIR, but could not unmount $MP"
  rmdir "$MP" 2>/dev/null
  rm -f "$STATE/$(mkid "$DIR")"
  echo "2ram: $DIR unloaded, ram data written back"
}

do_restore(){
  if is_bound; then
    umount "$DIR" || die "$DIR busy, nothing changed"
  fi
  if mountpoint -q "$MP"; then
    umount "$MP" || die "could not unmount $MP"
  fi
  rmdir "$MP" 2>/dev/null
  rm -f "$STATE/$(mkid "$DIR")"
  echo "2ram: $DIR restored to original mounts"
}

do_list(){
  local f
  for f in "$STATE"/*; do
    [ -e "$f" ] || continue
    . "$f"
    if is_bound; then echo "$DIR  $MP  $SIZE  bound"; else echo "$DIR  $MP  $SIZE  NOT-BOUND"; fi
  done
}

case "${1:-}" in
  ""|-h|--help) usage;;
  list) do_list;;
  unload|restore)
    cmd=$1; shift
    [ -n "${1:-}" ] || usage
    if [ "$1" = "all" ]; then
      for f in "$STATE"/*; do
        [ -e "$f" ] || continue
        . "$f"
        "do_$cmd"
      done
    else
      d=$(realpath -m -- "$1")
      f="$STATE/$(mkid "$d")"
      [ -e "$f" ] || die "not managed by 2ram: $d"
      . "$f"
      "do_$cmd"
    fi;;
  *) do_load "$@";;
esac
EOF
chmod 755 /usr/local/bin/2ram

Usage:

2ram /data/folder1
2ram /data/folder1 4M
2ram /data/folder1 2G
2ram unload /data/folder1
2ram restore /data/folder1
2ram list
Load: the ramdisk is mounted, the folder is copied in and verified with diff -rq, and only then is the bind mount placed over the folder. Any failure before the bind leaves everything unchanged. The original data stays untouched underneath the bind.
unload: the bind is unmounted, then rsync -a --delete copies ram to the real folder and a checksum dry-run verifies it. On any failure it re-binds the ram copy, so nothing is lost. It needs rsync.
restore: the bind and ramdisk are unmounted and the folder returns to its pre-2ram state. Changes made in ram are discarded.
Mount check: restore and unload only unmount DIR when its device matches the 2ram ramdisk. This means a pre-existing mount on the folder is never unmounted by mistake.
Open files: processes holding files open in the folder keep using the old files until they reopen them.

Answer: /usr/local/bin/2ram created, executable, with load, unload, restore, and list.

